      title: "How to Read a Contemporary Poem",
      body: `## A practical guide

You will meet many poems after this course that no teacher has explained, by poets no one has yet written about. How should you read them? Here is a practical method, drawing on everything you have learned in the concentration. It works for poems of any period, but it is especially useful for contemporary poems, which often lack the rhyme and meter that make older poems easier to hear.

## 1. Read it aloud, twice

Read the poem aloud, slowly, from beginning to end, without stopping to figure anything out. Then read it again. Poems are made of sound; you will understand more than you expect simply by hearing them. Notice where your voice wants to pause, speed up, or slow down. Notice which words sound strange, beautiful, or harsh.

## 2. Notice the line

In free verse, the line break is one of the poet's most important tools. Where does each line end? Does it end at a natural pause (end-stopped) or break in the middle of a phrase (enjambed)? What word sits at the end of each line, in the position of emphasis? Williams broke "wheel / barrow" to make us see the parts. Brooks ended her lines on "We" to make the young men's swagger audible. Ask of every line break: why here?

## 3. Look for form

Even poems that look free often have hidden patterns. Count the lines and stanzas. Are there repeated words, refrains, or rhymes, even slant ones? Is it a sonnet (fourteen lines), a villanelle (refrains), a ghazal (couplets ending in the same word), a golden shovel (a hidden poem down the margin)? Look at the shape on the page. Form is never only decoration; it shapes what the poem can say, as Bishop's villanelle shapes her grief.

## 4. Who is speaking, to whom, and when?

Every poem has a speaker, who may or may not be the poet. Ask who is speaking (a persona, like Browning's Duke or Hughes's mother; the poet; a collective "we"); to whom (the reader, a lover, the dead, God, no one); and in what situation (a deathbed, a lunch hour, a fishing boat). Many poems become clear the moment you identify the dramatic situation. Notice shifts: when does the speaker change tone, address someone new, or turn?

## 5. Find the turn

Most poems, like sonnets, turn somewhere: from description to reflection, from past to present, from question to answer, from one feeling to another. Find the turn and ask what changes. In Frost's "Mending Wall," it comes when the speaker asks why good fences make good neighbors; in Lowell's "Skunk Hour," when the town becomes the mind; in O'Hara's "The Day Lady Died," when the newspaper appears.

## 6. Follow the images and allusions

List the poem's images. Do they form patterns—of light and dark, of water, of falling? Do they echo images from other poems? Many contemporary poems allude to earlier ones, as Lowell's "I myself am hell" alludes to Milton, or Hayes's golden shovel to Brooks. You will catch more allusions the more you read. When a phrase sounds like a quotation, search for it.

## 7. Let it be difficult

Some poems, like Ashbery's, resist a single meaning. That is not a failure of the reader or the poem. Keats's "negative capability," which you met in POE 210—"being in uncertainties, Mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact & reason"—is the right attitude. Ask what the poem makes you feel and notice, even if you cannot paraphrase it.

## 8. Write it down

Finally, write a few sentences about the poem: what it is about, how it works, and what moved you. Quote specific words and lines. The act of writing will clarify your reading, and show you what you still need to understand.

## The habit of attention

All of these steps come down to one thing: attention. Bishop let the fish go because she looked at it long enough to see it. Whitman lay in the grass and saw the universe in a single blade. Dickinson heard a fly at a deathbed. Poetry trains us to pay that kind of attention, to language and to the world. The French philosopher Simone Weil, whose essay on the *Iliad* you met in POE 220, wrote that "attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity." It is what poems ask of us, and what they give.`,
