      body: `## The laureates

Since 1986, the Library of Congress has appointed a **Poet Laureate Consultant in Poetry**, the national poet of the United States. The list of laureates offers a map of contemporary American poetry. It has included Robert Penn Warren (the first), Richard Wilbur, Rita Dove, Robert Pinsky, Billy Collins, Louise Glück, Natasha Trethewey, Juan Felipe Herrera, Tracy K. Smith, Joy Harjo, and Ada Limón, among others. A few of them show the range of the art today.

**Rita Dove** (born 1952) won the Pulitzer Prize in 1987 for *Thomas and Beulah*, a sequence of poems telling the lives of her grandparents, who moved from the South to Akron, Ohio, during the Great Migration you read about in Lesson IV. In 1993 she became the first African American to hold the title of Poet Laureate. Her poetry joins a novelist's interest in ordinary lives to a musician's ear and a formalist's craft.

**Louise Glück** (1943–2023) wrote spare, intense poems, often drawing on Greek myth, about loss, family, and the self. Her book *The Wild Iris* (1992), in which flowers in a garden, the gardener, and God speak in turn, won the Pulitzer Prize. In 2020 she received the Nobel Prize in Literature, "for her unmistakable poetic voice that with austere beauty makes individual existence universal."

**Natasha Trethewey** (born 1966), the daughter of a Black mother and a white father in Mississippi, where their marriage was illegal when she was born, won the Pulitzer Prize in 2008 for *Native Guard*, which includes poems about her murdered mother and about the forgotten Black Union soldiers who guarded a fort off the Mississippi coast during the Civil War. She uses traditional forms—sonnets, ghazals, villanelles, pantoums—to recover buried history.

**Tracy K. Smith** (born 1972) won the Pulitzer Prize in 2012 for *Life on Mars*, an elegy for her father, an engineer who worked on the Hubble Space Telescope, which ranges across science fiction, David Bowie, and the vastness of space.

## The golden shovel

One recent invention shows how the tradition renews itself. In 2010, the poet **Terrance Hayes** (born 1971) published "The Golden Shovel," a poem built on Gwendolyn Brooks's "We Real Cool," which you read about in Lesson IV. Hayes took each line of Brooks's poem and used each of its words, in order, as the last word of a line in his own poem. Reading down the right-hand margin of Hayes's poem, you read Brooks's poem. The **golden shovel** has become a popular form, and in 2017, for the centenary of Brooks's birth, an anthology gathered golden shovels by dozens of poets. It is a perfect image of tradition as Eliot described it in POE 301: the new poem carries the old one within it, and changes how we read it.

## Poetry in public

American poetry today lives in many places. It lives in books and magazines, in universities and creative-writing programs, which now produce thousands of graduates a year. It lives in performance: in poetry slams, which began in Chicago in the 1980s; in spoken-word poetry, close to hip-hop; and on stages from the Nuyorican Poets Café to national television. It lives online, in social-media poetry that reaches millions of readers. And it lives at moments of public ceremony. Robert Frost read at John F. Kennedy's inauguration in 1961; Maya Angelou read "On the Pulse of Morning" at Bill Clinton's in 1993; and at Joe Biden's in January 2021, the twenty-two-year-old **Amanda Gorman**, the youngest inaugural poet in American history, read "The Hill We Climb," two weeks after a mob had attacked the Capitol behind her.

## Why poetry matters

In 1955, near the end of his life, William Carlos Williams wrote a long love poem to his wife, "Asphodel, That Greeny Flower," which contains some of the most quoted lines about poetry in American literature. Poems, he says, offer no news; and yet men die miserably every day for lack of what is found there. (The poem is in copyright; read it in his *Collected Poems*.) Whatever form it takes, poetry offers something that nothing else does: language at its most concentrated, attentive, and alive, holding experience still long enough for us to see it, and to see ourselves in it.`,
